[{:db/id 17592186198401 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-93856fba-8ee0-5cd2-b018-66154e6d6da5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-93856fba-8ee0-5cd2-b018-66154e6d6da5" :question/id #uuid "93856fba-8ee0-5cd2-b018-66154e6d6da5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161909" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b2f6d99ad589e6f93ddbaf260c75b53c265dfdaa1085d69cece362038f6e251e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,{z}^{2},\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,{z}^{2},y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower hemisphere ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q={z}^{2}=1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d74e471a-30e4-5206-9122-33818f0d279a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d74e471a-30e4-5206-9122-33818f0d279a" :answer-field/id #uuid "d74e471a-30e4-5206-9122-33818f0d279a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4c0bcbe0-535b-55f2-b9a8-588a01b4cff2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-18886f3c-0b4c-5fa3-95eb-8b2ae4357f06" "answer-24abe7ff-171f-51ce-9d3a-a696b7da2e15" "answer-9fbe5889-0cf0-5596-9144-1cb7762ac625" "answer-0e7f3b37-9248-5413-9c81-35a6b66aec61" "answer-4c0bcbe0-535b-55f2-b9a8-588a01b4cff2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18886f3c-0b4c-5fa3-95eb-8b2ae4357f06" :answer/id #uuid "18886f3c-0b4c-5fa3-95eb-8b2ae4357f06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24abe7ff-171f-51ce-9d3a-a696b7da2e15" :answer/id #uuid "24abe7ff-171f-51ce-9d3a-a696b7da2e15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fbe5889-0cf0-5596-9144-1cb7762ac625" :answer/id #uuid "9fbe5889-0cf0-5596-9144-1cb7762ac625" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}-y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e7f3b37-9248-5413-9c81-35a6b66aec61" :answer/id #uuid "0e7f3b37-9248-5413-9c81-35a6b66aec61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c0bcbe0-535b-55f2-b9a8-588a01b4cff2" :answer/id #uuid "4c0bcbe0-535b-55f2-b9a8-588a01b4cff2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186401309 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,y,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-9$ and the plane $z=y-x.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199468 :tutorial/content "Remember that when we measure the flux of a vector field through a *closed* surface, we always calculate with respect to an outward-pointing unit normal vector.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\langle x, \\,0, \\, -y^2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the conical solid shown below. \n\n![](images/0b/0be83ceada68d0b56be4872f21652a7d18932ff059ea704576ca3cce892fa87b.png)\n\n \n\nLet's calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$ \n\nFirst, since $S$ is a closed surface, we have that\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 2 - \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$ is oriented *upward*, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = 0$ is oriented *downward*.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- First, we consider the upper cone $S_1.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = 0, \\qquad\nR = -y^2.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+(-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Next, we consider the lower plane $S_2.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,-1 \\rangle.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨x,0,-{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the entire surface is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198486 :activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
